# Day 08 – Cloud Server Setup: Docker, Nginx & Web Deployment

Today I learned about cloud server deployment using AWS EC2, SSH, Docker, and Nginx. I also explored security groups, web access, and Nginx log management.

## 1. Connect to the Cloud Server

```bash
ssh -i your-key.pem ubuntu@<your-instance-public-ip>
```

Connects to an Ubuntu EC2 instance using SSH and a private key.

## 2. Update the System

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
```

Updates the package list and installs available system updates.

## 3. Install Docker

```bash
sudo apt install docker.io -y
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo systemctl status docker
```

Installs Docker, starts the service, enables it at startup, and checks its status.

## 4. Install Nginx

```bash
sudo apt install nginx -y
sudo systemctl start nginx
sudo systemctl enable nginx
sudo systemctl status nginx
```

Installs Nginx and ensures the web server is running.

## 5. Configure Security Groups

Configure the EC2 security group with these inbound rules:

| Protocol | Port | Purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| SSH | 22 | Remote server access |
| HTTP | 80 | Webpage access |

For security, restrict SSH access to your own IP address whenever possible.

## 6. Verify the Nginx Webpage

Open the following URL in a browser:

```text
http://<your-instance-public-ip>
```

Replace the placeholder with the actual public IP address of your instance.

If Nginx is running and network access is configured correctly, the default Nginx welcome page should appear.

## 7. Check Nginx Logs

### Access Logs

```bash
sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/nginx/access.log
```

Displays the latest 50 access-log entries.

### Error Logs

```bash
sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/nginx/error.log
```

Displays the latest 50 error-log entries.

## 8. Save Logs to a File

```bash
sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/nginx/access.log > ~/nginx-logs.txt
cat ~/nginx-logs.txt
```

Saves the latest access-log entries to `nginx-logs.txt` and displays the saved contents.

## 9. Download the Log File

Run this command from your local computer in a separate terminal:

```bash
scp -i your-key.pem ubuntu@<your-instance-public-ip>:~/nginx-logs.txt .
```

Copies the log file from the EC2 instance to your local directory. Replace the key filename and public IP with your actual values.

## 10. What I Learned Today

- How to connect to a cloud server using SSH.
- How to install and manage Docker and Nginx.
- How to configure security groups for HTTP access.
- How to verify a web server using its public IP address.
- How to extract and save logs for troubleshooting.

